WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.360
Evidence work does not end after initial assessment or authorization support.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:09.620
Continuous monitoring documentation shows whether the security posture remains current as

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.280
the system changes.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.020
It may include vulnerability scans, configuration changes,

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.440
significant changes at a high level, incident records, access reviews, patch status,

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.580
risk updates, control evidence refreshes, operational metrics,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.320
and responses to assessor or agency review questions.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.300
The key concept is currency.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.160
Historical approval does not prove today's posture.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.300
New vulnerabilities may be discovered.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.440
Cloud resources may change.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.180
Access may drift.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.240
Incidents may reveal gaps.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.140
Dependencies may evolve.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.600
Controls may stop operating as intended.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.140
Continuous monitoring documentation helps stakeholders understand what has changed,

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:55.400
what remains effective, what requires remediation, and what risk is being carried forward.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:00.340
Continuous monitoring records should align with the same scope and boundary story used in

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.920
the core package.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.540
If the inventory changes, diagrams and evidence may need review.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.320
If a significant architectural change occurs,

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.940
control narratives and inherited responsibility assumptions may need updates.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.160
If a vulnerability trend worsens,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.580
remediation documentation and risk reporting should reflect that.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.600
Monitoring evidence is only useful when it connects back to the system being assessed.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:28.660
The best continuous monitoring documentation reflects real operations.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.240
It should come from scans, tickets, logs, reviews, change records, incident processes,

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.580
and control evidence refreshes that teams actually use.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.160
It should not be a historical snapshot copied forward indefinitely.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.220
The purpose is to keep security, compliance, engineering,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.960
and risk stakeholders aligned with the live system, not with last year's approval story.
